\title{Fractal Dimension of Search Trees:\\Ambient Bounds, Periodic Realization, and the Separation of Geometry from Cost}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a normalized fractal dimension for successful prefixes in a bounded-branching search tree. In the binary case, let $N(n)$ be the number of depth-$n$ prefixes that can still extend to a terminal solution. The finite estimate is $d_n=\log_2N(n)/n$, and the upper entropy dimension is $D=\limsup_{n\to\infty}d_n$. Every binary profile satisfies the sharp ambient bound $D\leq1$, so no super-unit regime exists under this normalization. If exactly $r$ levels are free in every period of length $m$, then $D=r/m$, and the finite estimate equals this value exactly at every complete-period depth. Hence every rational dimension in $[0,1]$ is realized, while codimension is the density of forced levels. Finally, for every rational $d\in[0,1]$ and natural number $L$, an abstract search instance can have dimension $d$ and designated shortest terminal length $L$. Dimension therefore measures exponential abundance of viable prefixes, not terminal length or traversal cost. We present exact counting algorithms, numerical benchmarks, applications, and directions for a policy-sensitive theory of search.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Reasoning, planning, synthesis, and diagnosis can all be represented by rooted trees. A node records a partial sequence of decisions, and its children represent admissible next moves. Some prefixes remain extendable to a terminal solution; others are dead ends. The viable part of such a tree can thin out and repeat patterns across scales, suggesting the use of entropy and fractal dimension.

A tempting slogan is that higher dimension means greater difficulty. Two distinctions are needed before that slogan can be made precise. First, dimension is relative to an ambient metric and normalization. A normalized binary path space itself has dimension $1$, so none of its subsets can have larger dimension. Second, geometric abundance does not encode the order in which nodes are visited, where failures are recognized, or where terminal solutions occur.

We develop the elementary theory that resolves these points. We prove the universal unit upper bound, compute periodic models exactly, realize every rational dimension in the unit interval, identify codimension with the density of forced decisions, and show that dimension alone leaves designated terminal length completely unconstrained.

\section{Binary search profiles}

Let $\{0,1\}^n$ be the binary words of length $n$, and let $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N}$ be the infinite binary sequences. For distinct $x,y\in\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N}$, let $\ell(x,y)$ be the length of their longest common prefix and define
\[
  \rho(x,y)=2^{-\ell(x,y)}, \qquad \rho(x,x)=0.
\]
This ultrametric makes two routes close when they share many initial decisions. A cylinder determined by a word of length $n$ has diameter $2^{-n}$.

\begin{definition}[Successful-prefix profile]
A binary successful-prefix profile is a family $S=(S_n)_{n\geq0}$ with $S_n\subseteq\{0,1\}^n$. Its members are interpreted as depth-$n$ prefixes that remain extendable to a terminal solution. Naturally, one may require prefix closure: every prefix of a successful word is successful. Write $N_S(n)=|S_n|$.
\end{definition}

For a nonempty profile and $n\geq1$, define the finite-depth estimate
\[
 d_S(n)=\frac{\log_2N_S(n)}{n}.
\]
The normalized upper search dimension and codimension are
\[
 D(S)=\limsup_{n\to\infty}d_S(n),
 \qquad \codim(S)=1-D(S).
\]
For regular profiles this is the upper box or entropy dimension of the corresponding path set. The upper limit accommodates aperiodic profiles whose estimates oscillate. The full binary tree has $N(n)=2^n$ and dimension $1$; a single forced route has $N(n)=1$ and dimension $0$. Empty profiles require a separate logarithmic convention and are not needed below.

\section{The ambient boundary}

\begin{theorem}[Ambient Bound]
Every binary successful-prefix profile $S$ satisfies
\[
D(S)\leq1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At depth $n$, the successful words are a subset of all $2^n$ binary words. Thus $N_S(n)\leq2^n$, and whenever the estimate is defined,
\[
 d_S(n)=\frac{\log_2N_S(n)}{n}
 \leq\frac{\log_2(2^n)}{n}=1.
\]
Taking the upper limit proves the result. Equality is attained by the full binary tree.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No super-unit regime]
For every profile $S$ and every real $\varepsilon>0$, one has $D(S)\neq1+\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The proposed value is greater than $1$, contrary to the Ambient Bound.
\end{proof}

The obstruction is definitional, not statistical. A criterion that declares a normalized binary search hard precisely when $D>1$ has no instances. Values above $1$ require a changed metric, normalization, or ambient space.

\section{Periodic pruning}

\begin{definition}[Periodically pruned search]
Fix $m\geq1$ and a set $R\subseteq\{0,1,\ldots,m-1\}$. A level $j\geq0$ is free when $j\bmod m\in R$ and forced otherwise. At each free level both binary extensions remain successful; at each forced level only one prescribed extension remains successful. The pattern repeats every $m$ levels.
\end{definition}

Put $r=|R|$ and let
\[
 F(n)=\bigl|\{j:0\leq j<n,\ j\bmod m\in R\}\bigr|
\]
be the number of free levels below depth $n$. Every free level doubles the viable count and every forced level preserves it, so
\[
 N(n)=2^{F(n)}, \qquad d(n)=\frac{F(n)}{n}.
\]
If $n=mk+s$ with $0\leq s<m$, then $F(n)=rk+O(1)$, where the bounded term counts free positions in the final partial period.

\begin{theorem}[Periodic Dimension]
A periodically pruned binary search with $r$ free levels in each period of length $m$ has dimension
\[
D=\frac rm.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Writing $n=mk+s$ gives
\[
 d(n)=\frac{F(n)}n=\frac{rk+O(1)}{mk+s}\longrightarrow\frac rm.
\]
Thus the ordinary limit exists and equals $r/m$, and so does the upper dimension.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rational Realization]
For natural numbers $p,q$ with $0\leq p\leq q$ and $q\geq1$, there exists a binary successful-prefix profile $S$ satisfying
\[
D(S)=\frac pq.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use period $q$ and choose exactly $p$ free residues. The Periodic Dimension Theorem gives the claim.
\end{proof}

The endpoints are included: no free residues produce a forced path of dimension $0$, while all residues free produce the full tree of dimension $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Period-Boundary Estimate]
For a periodic model with period $m$ and $r$ free levels per period, every $k\geq1$ satisfies
\[
 d(mk)=\frac rm.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
There are exactly $rk$ free levels in $k$ periods. Hence $N(mk)=2^{rk}$ and
\[
 d(mk)=\frac{\log_2(2^{rk})}{mk}=\frac rm.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Periodic Codimension]
For every rational $p/q\in[0,1]$, a periodic profile can be chosen such that
\[
 D=\frac pq, \qquad \codim=1-D=\frac{q-p}{q}.
\]
Its codimension is exactly the density of forced levels.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $p$ free and $q-p$ forced positions in a period of length $q$. The dimension is $p/q$, and subtraction gives $(q-p)/q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
Take $m=3$ and $R=\{0,1\}$. The dimension is $2/3$. At depth $12$, there are eight free levels, so
\[
 N(12)=2^8=256, \qquad d(12)=\frac{\log_2 256}{12}=\frac23.
\]
The ambient tree has $2^{12}=4096$ words at that depth.
\end{example}

\section{Terminal length is separate data}

The profile $S$ records prefix geometry but need not specify where terminal solutions are declared. We therefore isolate the missing coordinate.

\begin{definition}[Abstract search instance]
An abstract search instance is a pair $I=(S,L)$, where $S$ is a successful-prefix profile and $L\in\mathbb N$ is a designated shortest terminal length. Define $D(I)=D(S)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Independence of Dimension and Designated Length]
Let $p,q,L\in\mathbb N$ satisfy $p\leq q$ and $q\geq1$. There exists an abstract search instance $I=(S,L)$ such that
\[
D(S)=\frac pq
\]
and its designated shortest terminal length is exactly $L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Rational Realization, choose a periodic profile $S$ of dimension $p/q$. Pair it with the prescribed $L$. Since dimension depends only on $S$, the assignment of $L$ leaves it unchanged.
\end{proof}

Thus fixed dimension $1/2$, for example, can coexist with designated shortest length $10$, $1000$, or any natural number. This does not preclude correlations in a richer model; it proves that no correlation follows from successful-prefix dimension alone. A semantic law connecting terminal solutions to prefixes is indispensable.

\section{Algorithms and numerical benchmarks}

For exact periodic counting, write $n=mk+s$ with $0\leq s<m$. Then
\[
 F(n)=|R|k+|R\cap\{0,1,\ldots,s-1\}|.
\]
Return $N(n)=2^{F(n)}$ and $d(n)=F(n)/n$. A direct scan costs $O(n)$ time. With a table of cumulative free residues, preprocessing costs $O(m)$ time and space, after which each $F(n)$ query costs $O(1)$. Since $N(n)$ may require exponentially many bits to display, implementations can return the logarithmic count $F(n)$ instead.

To realize $p/q$, choose $m=q$ and $R=\{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}$. This uses $O(p)$ explicit storage, or constant storage if membership is represented by the inequality $j\bmod q<p$.

If exact counts are unavailable, let
\[
 P_n=\frac{N(n)}{2^n}
\]
be the survival probability of a uniformly sampled depth-$n$ word. Then
\[
 d(n)=1+\frac{\log_2P_n}{n}.
\]
Sampling estimates $P_n$, but rare survival causes high variance and zero-count difficulties. Periodic profiles supply exact calibration cases; confidence statements additionally require a probability model and concentration analysis.

\section{From abundance to cost}

If $N(n)=2^{dn+o(n)}$, then the ambient survival fraction is
\[
 \frac{N(n)}{2^n}=2^{-(1-d)n+o(n)}.
\]
This makes codimension a plausible rarity exponent. Independent uniform sampling would heuristically need about $2^{(1-d)n}$ trials to encounter a successful depth-$n$ word. It is not, however, a universal traversal theorem. Tree algorithms share prefix work, policies are nonuniform, and failure may be detected at different depths.

A cost theory must therefore add at least three ingredients:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item a traversal policy specifying visitation order;
\item a failure distribution specifying where unsuccessful branches terminate;
\item a terminal-depth law specifying where viable routes become complete solutions.
\end{enumerate}
Uniform extension hypotheses may connect levelwise abundance to accessibility. Mixing assumptions may justify probabilistic estimators. Without them, dimension remains geometric rather than algorithmic.

\section{Extensions and applications}

For a regular $b$-ary tree, the normalized estimate is
\[
 d_b(n)=\frac{\log_bN(n)}n.
\]
Because $N(n)\leq b^n$, the same ambient bound $D_b\leq1$ holds. When selected levels retain all $b$ choices and the others retain one, periodic free-level density again equals dimension.

Stationary ergodic pruning suggests a broader entropy-rate law: normalized dimension should equal a conditional entropy rate divided by $\log b$. Periodic pruning is the deterministic finite-memory benchmark. Aperiodic concatenations of increasingly long periodic blocks should yield distinct lower and upper dimensions, explaining the use of a limit superior.

The framework applies to partial derivations, program synthesis, planning, diagnosis, and game search. In each case it quantifies exponential abundance of viable partial states. Synthetic periodic profiles are useful for calibrating estimators, while application-specific cost conclusions require models of generation, prioritization, testing, and termination.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The unit upper bound corrects the notion of a super-unit hard regime. The periodic family then shows that the admissible interval is richly populated: every rational point occurs, exact finite estimates are available at complete periods, and codimension has the transparent meaning of forced-level density. The independence theorem marks the boundary of the geometry: terminal complexity cannot be recovered from prefix counts alone.

Five problems are immediate. First, prove an entropy-rate law for stationary ergodic pruning. Second, derive a dimension--cost theorem under unbiased breadth-first traversal and a uniform extension condition. Third, construct two computable policies on one fixed positive-dimensional tree with polynomial versus exponential discovery time. Fourth, realize prescribed rational lower and upper dimensions by oscillatory aperiodic pruning. Fifth, establish statistically consistent estimators for finite-state Markov pruning with explicit mixing corrections and exponentially decaying error probabilities.

\section{Conclusion}

Normalized binary successful-prefix dimension lies in $[0,1]$. A periodic model with $r$ free positions in period $m$ has dimension $r/m$, realizes every rational value in the unit interval, and reaches its limiting estimate exactly at complete-period depths. Codimension is the density of forced decisions. Nevertheless, any such dimension can coexist with any separately designated shortest terminal length. Dimension is therefore an entropy-like measure of viable-prefix geometry; a full theory of difficulty must join it to policy, failure structure, and terminal semantics.

