A bounded binary search is a tree of partial decisions. At depth $n$, let $N(n)$ count prefixes that can still reach a solution. The normalized finite estimate is $d_n=\log_2N(n)/n$. Begin by changing a periodic pattern and watching its exact dimension respond.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The core boundary is simple but decisive: $N(n)\leq2^n$, hence every normalized dimension satisfies $D\leq1$. There is no super-unit regime in a fixed binary ambient space.

